✅ Would Logs Be Automatically Collected If We Kept the Istio Sidecar in Datadog?
Yes, if Istio sidecar (istio-proxy) was still injected into the Datadog agent pod, the logs could be collected automatically, but there are some issues that make disabling the sidecar a better approach.

🔹 What Happens If Datadog Has an Istio Sidecar?
If the Datadog pod has an Istio sidecar, all its traffic, including log collection, will go through Istio’s mTLS encryption. This can cause two problems:

🚨 Problem 1: Datadog Cannot Reach Istio Logs Due to mTLS
When Datadog tries to collect logs from other services (like istio-proxy, istiod, or service-a), the traffic goes through the Istio proxy.
Istio encrypts this traffic using mTLS, which means the Datadog agent might not be able to properly read or forward the logs.
🚨 Problem 2: Datadog Logs Themselves Get Captured By Istio
Since Datadog runs inside the Istio service mesh, all logs and metrics that Datadog collects must pass through the Istio proxy.
This means that Istio logs will also contain Datadog logs, leading to duplicate log entries or unnecessary overhead.
✅ Why Disabling the Istio Sidecar for Datadog Helps
By disabling the Istio sidecar in Datadog, we solve both problems:

Datadog can collect logs directly from the Kubernetes log paths (/var/log/pods/...).
Logs are collected without interference from Istio’s mTLS, preventing conflicts or failures.
Datadog can collect logs from Istio components (istiod, istio-ingressgateway, istio-proxy) properly.